    }
  ],
  "core_mechanism": "Freeze a public DreamerV3- or TD-MPC2-style action-conditioned latent world model at FP16 (an inferred pilot anchor, not a prior claim or a new backbone), add no foundation-model pretraining, and use the existing uniform-affine PyTorch torch.ao.quantization path as the numerical weight/activation PTQ operator; this numerical path is an emulation boundary and is not evidence that an A100 has a deployable W4A4 kernel. Let G be the deterministic union, over every declared grid cell, of every eligible Linear or Conv weight tensor W_g and its activation output A_g reached by the planner's scoring forward path; optimizer state and planner-only code are not quantized, each g has a measured per-site storage record m_g(b), and require |G| >= 1. From named DeepMind Control Suite (DMC) visual continuous-control tasks, collect disjoint self-built calibration and evaluation streams, with each record r storing the complete planner replay state q_t (including z_t and any recurrent/history/cache/normalization/RNG state needed to replay the scoring path), a planner-generated candidate set U_t, and its horizon, update schedule, receding-horizon convention, and tie rule. Use the pre-registered grid H={max(1,floor(H_native/2)), H_native, min(2H_native,H_episode)}, N={max(1,floor(N_native/2)), N_native, 2N_native}, M={max(1,floor(M0/4)), max(1,floor(M0/2)), M0}, S={s_native, random shooting, CEM}, E={e_native, one-step}, and T={stable-index, seeded-random}, removing duplicate cells; define M0 as the minimum, over the non-M base cells, of the largest positive per-cell trajectory count fitting the fixed A100 pilot envelope, and let grid-cell weights omega_c default to 1/|C| unless deployment frequencies are preregistered. Let B={(FP16,FP16), W8A8, W8A4, W4A8, W4A4} be the analysis bit-pair set, and let B_deploy be the subset with a measured backend-supported weight/activation implementation; fake-quant-only pairs may be profiled numerically but cannot enter deployment Pareto curves or the lowest supported deployment target. For each g and b in B, quantize only g with the same observer/calibration data, keep every other site FP16, rerun the identical candidate set under cell c, and define d_g,c(r;b) as the normalized pairwise candidate-order disagreement from FP16 using the explicit tie rule below. The central object is the rollout-ranking influence profile I_g,c(b)=average_r in D_c of d_g,c(r;b) and I_g(b)=sum_c omega_c I_g,c(b), a dimensionless counterfactual estimand of how much site g changes planner ordering over the observed rollout regime; finite accumulators compute the stated finite-sample estimator, subject to ordinary numerical rounding, rather than approximating an unchosen Shapley value. The heterogeneous companion retains each full profile vector P_g=(I_g,c(b)) across cells as its heterogeneity representation (no unbound clustering rule) and chooses b_g by minimizing sum_g omega_g I_g(b_g) subject to the system-level PeakBytes(b_1,...,b_|G|) <= M_mem, where PeakBytes is measured on the materialized end-to-end graph and includes resident weights, activation lifetimes and shared tensors, scales/zero-points, weight copies, and backend workspace; m_g(b) remains a diagnostic per-site record, not a quantity to sum as though sites were disjoint. The resulting all-sites assignment is evaluated end-to-end on disjoint episodes for every feasible grid cell and memory target, reporting rank agreement, first-action agreement, closed-loop return or success, peak memory, and control frequency; the influence profile is therefore an empirical allocation signal rather than a claim of global optimality. A matched intervention keeps candidate pools, start states, bit histogram, exact measured PeakBytes, memory target, planner schedule, receding horizon convention, and tie rule fixed while sampling only non-identity assignments with the same bit-pair histogram and exact measured PeakBytes; if no such assignment exists, the negative control is recorded as unavailable. This control tests attribution under matched conditions but does not prove causality or guarantee a downstream gain. The evidence boundary is explicit: arxiv:2602.02110v1 is connector-verified host_recall with fulltext H/html_arxiv, arxiv:2608.24855v1 is arxiv-retrieved with fulltext T3/html_arxiv, and arxiv:2607.28405v1 is connector-verified host_recall but has no fulltext cache entry in this run and is used only as an abstract-only adjacent comparison; none is introduced as the backbone.",
  "core_mechanism_reasoning": "BLOCK 1 — PREMISES ledger\nP1. Candidate action-sequence ordering is the operational decision variable, not merely a proxy for latent or observation reconstruction — reason: the planner selects actions by comparing candidate scores.\nP2. A site-only quantization intervention with the candidate pool, FP16 weights elsewhere, and planner inputs held fixed isolates a useful marginal decision influence (untested — falsification target) — reason: it creates a matched counterfactual while preserving the downstream scoring path.\nP3. The influence profile transfers sufficiently from FP16 planner visitation to quantized receding-horizon visitation for allocation to help (untested — falsification target) — reason: the calibration stream is generated by the same control loop, but low-bit errors can move later states off that stream.\nP4. Site-level marginal influence is sufficiently compositional for a memory-constrained assignment to be useful even though joint quantization can interact (untested — falsification target) — reason: the single-site table makes the pilot tractable, while the all-sites evaluation tests the approximation.\nP5. Existing public weights expose the eligible weight tensors and activation outputs needed for numerical PTQ, and the chosen DMC model/planner path can be run at FP16 and mixed W/A precision — reason: the proposal uses model-internal access, not gradients, closed-model APIs, or a new training corpus.\nOBSERVATION-MODEL: D_cal is sampled as stratified trajectories from named DMC tasks under the FP16 native, random-shooting, and CEM schedules, with fixed start-state and random-seed manifests; it is not unbiased over all reachable latent states, is biased toward the FP16 planner's visitation and candidate distribution, and is kept disjoint from D_eval.\nESTIMAND: for each site g and bit pair b, I_g(b) estimates the expected normalized pairwise candidate-order discordance between the FP16 planner and the model with only g quantized, over the uniform-weighted cells C and the records D_c drawn from this stated DMC/planner process; it is conditional on that process, not an unbiased population average over all states, tasks, or backbones.\nBLOCK 2 — NAIVE-BASELINE AUDIT\nNaive version: run uniform-affine PTQ, select each site's weight and activation bits by lowest layer-output reconstruction error on short calibration trajectories, and spend the remaining memory budget on the sites with the smallest local error.\nIt fails under branch (i): this version silently assumes that local reconstruction and calibration-distribution fidelity predict candidate ranking under recurrent action-conditioned rollouts, a premise that is false as a general rule because planner-induced states and candidate orderings change with horizon. RankCal confronts that failure with site-only counterfactual ranking influence; although counterfactual intervention is a standard diagnostic tool, the domain-specific structure is the adaptive candidate pool plus receding-horizon latent feedback under a fixed mixed W/A memory budget, so this is an application-grade method rather than a claim of a new generic causal estimator.\nBLOCK 3 — DESIGN RATIONALE\nI kept one anchor and deferred the cross-backbone transfer gap and the standalone local-proxy audit because the former is a validation axis and the latter is a negative-control comparison that can run through the same anchor machinery; adding either as a separate leg would make the paper broader without making the method more complete.\nI selected C00 from the anchor alternates because its fixed-model counterfactual measurement object directly matches candidate-order influence, while the heterogeneous companion turns that object into a runnable memory-constrained W/A allocation.\nThe method's central object is defined as a site-isolated estimand and the final full-model evaluation, not a claimed Shapley or globally additive optimum; this keeps the C00 compression step honest when joint quantization interactions violate additivity.\nI rejected uniform local-reconstruction, generic activation-sensitivity, and module/horizon-only routes because they regress to the established n0-n2 PTQ lineage or repeat the n3 DINO-WM sweep; DINO-WM and LeFlow are full-text comparators, QuantWAMs is connector-verified but abstract-only and adjacent, and DreamerV3/TD-MPC2 remain explicitly inferred pilot anchors.",
  "core_mechanism_steps": "1. Select one publicly available DreamerV3- or TD-MPC2-style action-conditioned latent world-model checkpoint as the inferred pilot anchor and one named DeepMind Control Suite task family; freeze its FP16 weights, reproduce the native planner path, and write a reference configuration containing H_native, N_native, s_native, e_native, and the native tie rule. Bind tau=0 by default (exact score equality) with stable candidate-index resolution for ties; any nonzero tolerance must be preregistered before D_cal. Inputs: public checkpoint and DMC environment. Outputs: reproducible FP16 model, planner, and configuration; no DINO-WM or LeWM backbone is introduced.\n2. Trace the PyTorch scoring forward graph over every declared grid cell and enumerate G as the deterministic union of the eligible Linear/Conv weight tensors W_g plus activation outputs A_g; require |G| >= 1. Attach the existing uniform-affine observers without changing the FP16 path, and reject the manifest if the observer attachment changes any FP16 planner score or candidate order. Inputs: frozen model, planner code, and declared grid. Outputs: site manifest, per-site weight/activation access, and the FP16 memory accounting record; FP16 planner scores remain unchanged only after this no-op check passes.\n3. Build disjoint D_cal and D_eval streams from the named DMC tasks using the grid C over M, N, S, H, E, and T; define D_c={r in D_cal: cell(r)=c}. For each calibration record save the complete planner replay state q_t=(z_t plus any recurrent/history/cache/normalization/RNG state needed for replay), the FP16-generated U_t, and cell metadata, while D_eval contains fresh environment episodes with no shared episode identifiers. Require each evaluated D_c to be nonempty; otherwise mark the cell unavailable rather than forming a zero-denominator mean. Inputs: DMC, FP16 planner, M0 selection rule, and fixed seed manifest. Outputs: stratified calibration records and held-out episodes.\n4. For every g, b, and c, run site-only PTQ using the same observer data, replace only g in the FP16 checkpoint, and score the identical U_t through the declared imagined latent rollout. Let K_r=|U_t|; for K_r >= 2 set d_g,c(r;b)=[sum_{i<j} 1{ord_tau(s_g,b(u_i),s_g,b(u_j)) != ord_tau(s_FP16(u_i),s_FP16(u_j))}]/binom(K_r,2), where ord_tau treats scores within tau as a tie and resolves it by candidate index; for K_r < 2 set d_g,c(r;b)=0 and record the zero-pair count. The intervention propagates through every later latent transition and planner score, so its output is a changed candidate ranking and possibly a changed first action, not just a changed tensor statistic. Inputs: site manifest and D_cal. Outputs: cell-level rank-disagreement tables.\n5. Aggregate the tables into I_g,c(b) and I_g(b), retain the full profile vector across C, and compute local layer-output error and calibration-distribution fidelity only as named comparison signals. Inputs: rank-disagreement tables and omega_c. Outputs: the finite-sample counterfactual influence profile and comparator table used by the allocator.\n6. For each feasible M_mem in the pre-registered Pareto memory sweep, retain the full profile vector as the heterogeneity representation and solve the stated memory-constrained assignment over B_deploy with the measured system-level PeakBytes(b_1,...,b_|G|) <= M_mem; if no assignment is feasible, mark that cell/target infeasible and report no downstream metric rather than silently substituting another map. Inputs: I_g,c(b), PeakBytes, B_deploy, omega_g, and M_mem. Outputs: a per-site weight/activation bit map; materializing that map changes which numerical operators feed future latent rollouts and therefore which action sequence the planner selects.\n7. Materialize the all-sites mixed-precision checkpoint and run the full planner end-to-end for every feasible c and M_mem on D_eval, allowing the selected candidate set and receding-horizon execution to follow each cell's declared convention. Inputs: FP16 checkpoint, bit map, and fresh episodes. Outputs: candidate-order agreement, first-action agreement, closed-loop return or success, peak memory, and control-frequency measurements.\n8. Run matched negative controls by sampling non-identity assignments with the same bit-pair histogram and exact measured PeakBytes as the RankCal assignment, and by using the local-reconstruction allocator while preserving the same M_mem, candidate/update grid, receding-horizon convention, tie rule, planner seeds, and evaluation episodes. If no non-identity assignment satisfies both matches, record the control as unavailable; interpret an observed difference as a matched diagnostic, not a proof that I_g is the sole causal factor. Inputs: the same D_eval episodes and matched resource budget. Outputs: downstream control metrics that test whether the profile, rather than resource amount alone, determines the benefit.\n9. Report the full memory-versus-control-fidelity Pareto curves and the result of the randomized mapping; choose a deployment map only by the requested feasible M_mem target and retain all feasible grid cells in the paper's claim, rather than selecting a favorable horizon, calibration size, planner schedule, execution convention, or tie rule after observing outcomes. Inputs: all evaluation and control tables. Outputs: an auditable method specification and falsifiable claim.",
  "falsification_prediction": "Minimal experiment: on one public FP16 DreamerV3- or TD-MPC2-style checkpoint (inferred pilot anchor) and one DeepMind Control Suite visual continuous-control task, compare RankCal with uniform-bit and local-reconstruction allocation at matched peak memory across the full C grid. If the mechanism works, the primary downstream metric, closed-loop task-success rate, will be higher for RankCal than for the matched controls, while candidate-order and first-action agreement will degrade less in the longer-horizon cells. The single load-bearing variable is I_g(b), the site-only rollout-ranking influence profile; randomize its mapping to bit pairs while preserving the bit histogram, memory, candidate/update schedule, receding-horizon convention, and tie rule, then rerun fresh D_eval episodes. The negative control predicts that the randomized map's downstream task-success rate returns to the matched local-reconstruction or uniform-PTQ baseline, not merely that I_g changes; if the benefit survives the randomized mapping, the proposed influence signal is not the mechanism carrying the result.",
  "compute_budget": "GPU: estimated 12 GPU-days on one NSCC ASPIRE2A A100-SXM4-40GB for the one-task pilot, site-only probes, full nuisance grid, matched controls, and held-out closed-loop evaluation; an optional second DMC task family would add an estimated 6 GPU-days. API: $0 for the full campaign because model inference, calibration, and evaluation are local and no paid API judge is required. No GPU experiments are run during this proposal phase.",
