D:\Downloads\Final Year Project\ideaspark_run\world-model-quantization\phase0\arxiv_phase0.json-603- {
D:\Downloads\Final Year Project\ideaspark_run\world-model-quantization\phase0\arxiv_phase0.json-604-  "title": "Cross-Epoch Adaptive Rollout Optimization for RL Post-Training",
D:\Downloads\Final Year Project\ideaspark_run\world-model-quantization\phase0\arxiv_phase0.json-605-  "title_norm": "cross epoch adaptive rollout optimization for rl post training",
D:\Downloads\Final Year Project\ideaspark_run\world-model-quantization\phase0\arxiv_phase0.json:606:  "abstract": "LLM post-training often relies on reinforcement learning methods that sample multiple rollouts per prompt, yet most existing approaches use a fixed rollout budget for every prompt, despite large differences in the training signal different prompts provide. In this paper, we study adaptive rollout allocation under a fixed global budget and formulate the problem as online resource allocation with prompt-level diminishing returns. Our method, CERO, maintains a Beta posterior over each prompt's success probability and uses the posterior expected Bernoulli variance as a Bayesian estimate of the value of additional rollouts. We use this estimate to construct a concave, saturating utility over cumulative allocations, yielding an objective in which decisions across prompts and epochs are coupled by the global budget. Since the resulting objective is temporally nonseparable, we derive a Fenchel-dual reformulation and update both prompt-level and budget-level dual variables via projected online gradient descent. Under fixed prompt utilities, we prove an $O(\\sqrt{K})$ regret bound against the offline allocation benchmark. Experiments on mathematical-reasoning problems show that CERO consistently outperforms GRPO across multiple open-weight LLMs and benchmarks, demonstrating that adaptive rollout budgeting can improve sample efficiency.",
D:\Downloads\Final Year Project\ideaspark_run\world-model-quantization\phase0\arxiv_phase0.json-607-  "year": 2026,
D:\Downloads\Final Year Project\ideaspark_run\world-model-quantization\phase0\arxiv_phase0.json-608-  "venue": "arXiv",
===== full candidate targeted fields =====
"1. Select one publicly available DreamerV3- or TD-MPC2-style action-conditioned latent world-model checkpoint as the inferred pilot anchor and one named DeepMind Control Suite task family; freeze its FP16 weights, reproduce the native planner path, and write a reference configuration containing H_native, N_native, s_native, e_native, and the native tie rule. Bind tau=0 by default (exact score equality) with stable candidate-index resolution for ties; any nonzero tolerance must be preregistered before D_cal. Inputs: public checkpoint and DMC environment. Outputs: reproducible FP16 model, planner, and configuration; no DINO-WM or LeWM backbone is introduced.\n2. Trace the PyTorch scoring forward graph over every declared grid cell and enumerate G as the deterministic union of the eligible Linear/Conv weight tensors W_g plus activation outputs A_g; require |G| >= 1. Attach the existing uniform-affine observers without changing the FP16 path, and reject the manifest if the observer attachment changes any FP16 planner score or candidate order. Inputs: frozen model, planner code, and declared grid. Outputs: site manifest, per-site weight/activation access, and the FP16 memory accounting record; FP16 planner scores remain unchanged only after this no-op check passes.\n3. Build disjoint D_cal and D_eval streams from the named DMC tasks using the grid C over M, N, S, H, E, and T; define D_c={r in D_cal: cell(r)=c}. For each calibration record save the complete planner replay state q_t=(z_t plus any recurrent/history/cache/normalization/RNG state needed for replay), the FP16-generated U_t, and cell metadata, while D_eval contains fresh environment episodes with no shared episode identifiers. Require each evaluated D_c to be nonempty; otherwise mark the cell unavailable rather than forming a zero-denominator mean. Inputs: DMC, FP16 planner, M0 selection rule, and fixed seed manifest. Outputs: stratified calibration records and held-out episodes.\n4. For every g, b, and c, run site-only PTQ using the same observer data, replace only g in the FP16 checkpoint, and score the identical U_t through the declared imagined latent rollout. Let K_r=|U_t|; for K_r >= 2 set d_g,c(r;b)=[sum_{i<j} 1{ord_tau(s_g,b(u_i),s_g,b(u_j)) != ord_tau(s_FP16(u_i),s_FP16(u_j))}]/binom(K_r,2), where ord_tau treats scores within tau as a tie and resolves it by candidate index; for K_r < 2 set d_g,c(r;b)=0 and record the zero-pair count. The intervention propagates through every later latent transition and planner score, so its output is a changed candidate ranking and possibly a changed first action, not just a changed tensor statistic. Inputs: site manifest and D_cal. Outputs: cell-level rank-disagreement tables.\n5. Aggregate the tables into I_g,c(b) and I_g(b), retain the full profile vector across C, and compute local layer-output error and calibration-distribution fidelity only as named comparison signals. Inputs: rank-disagreement tables and omega_c. Outputs: the finite-sample counterfactual influence profile and comparator table used by the allocator.\n6. For each feasible M_mem in the pre-registered Pareto memory sweep, retain the full profile vector as the heterogeneity representation and solve the stated memory-constrained assignment over B_deploy with the measured system-level PeakBytes(b_1,...,b_|G|) <= M_mem; if no assignment is feasible, mark that cell/target infeasible and report no downstream metric rather than silently substituting another map. Inputs: I_g,c(b), PeakBytes, B_deploy, omega_g, and M_mem. Outputs: a per-site weight/activation bit map; materializing that map changes which numerical operators feed future latent rollouts and therefore which action sequence the planner selects.\n7. Materialize the all-sites mixed-precision checkpoint and run the full planner end-to-end for every feasible c and M_mem on D_eval, allowing the selected candidate set and receding-horizon execution to follow each cell's declared convention. Inputs: FP16 checkpoint, bit map, and fresh episodes. Outputs: candidate-order agreement, first-action agreement, closed-loop return or success, peak memory, and control-frequency measurements.\n8. Run matched negative controls by sampling non-identity assignments with the same bit-pair histogram and exact measured PeakBytes as the RankCal assignment, and by using the local-reconstruction allocator while preserving the same M_mem, candidate/update grid, receding-horizon convention, tie rule, planner seeds, and evaluation episodes. If no non-identity assignment satisfies both matches, record the control as unavailable; interpret an observed difference as a matched diagnostic, not a proof that I_g is the sole causal factor. Inputs: the same D_eval episodes and matched resource budget. Outputs: downstream control metrics that test whether the profile, rather than resource amount alone, determines the benefit.\n9. Report the full memory-versus-control-fidelity Pareto curves and the result of the randomized mapping; choose a deployment map only by the requested feasible M_mem target and retain all feasible grid cells in the paper's claim, rather than selecting a favorable horizon, calibration size, planner schedule, execution convention, or tie rule after observing outcomes. Inputs: all evaluation and control tables. Outputs: an auditable method specification and falsifiable claim."
BLOCK 1 — PREMISES ledger
P1. Candidate action-sequence ordering is the operational decision variable, not merely a proxy for latent or observation reconstruction — reason: the planner selects actions by comparing candidate scores.
P2. A site-only quantization intervention with the candidate pool, FP16 weights elsewhere, and planner inputs held fixed isolates a useful marginal decision influence (untested — falsification target) — reason: it creates a matched counterfactual while preserving the downstream scoring path.
P3. The influence profile transfers sufficiently from FP16 planner visitation to quantized receding-horizon visitation for allocation to help (untested — falsification target) — reason: the calibration stream is generated by the same control loop, but low-bit errors can move later states off that stream.
P4. Site-level marginal influence is sufficiently compositional for a memory-constrained assignment to be useful even though joint quantization can interact (untested — falsification target) — reason: the single-site table makes the pilot tractable, while the all-sites evaluation tests the approximation.
P5. Existing public weights expose the eligible weight tensors and activation outputs needed for numerical PTQ, and the chosen DMC model/planner path can be run at FP16 and mixed W/A precision — reason: the proposal uses model-internal access, not gradients, closed-model APIs, or a new training corpus.
OBSERVATION-MODEL: D_cal is sampled as stratified trajectories from named DMC tasks under the FP16 native, random-shooting, and CEM schedules, with fixed start-state and random-seed manifests; it is not unbiased over all reachable latent states, is biased toward the FP16 planner's visitation and candidate distribution, and is kept disjoint from D_eval.
ESTIMAND: for each site g and bit pair b, I_g(b) estimates the expected normalized pairwise candidate-order discordance between the FP16 planner and the model with only g quantized, over the uniform-weighted cells C and the records D_c drawn from this stated DMC/planner process; it is conditional on that process, not an unbiased population average over all states, tasks, or backbones.
BLOCK 2 — NAIVE-BASELINE AUDIT
Naive version: run uniform-affine PTQ, select each site's weight and activation bits by lowest layer-output reconstruction error on short calibration trajectories, and spend the remaining memory budget on the sites with the smallest local error.
It fails under branch (i): this version silently assumes that local reconstruction and calibration-distribution fidelity predict candidate ranking under recurrent action-conditioned rollouts, a premise that is false as a general rule because planner-induced states and candidate orderings change with horizon. RankCal confronts that failure with site-only counterfactual ranking influence; although counterfactual intervention is a standard diagnostic tool, the domain-specific structure is the adaptive candidate pool plus receding-horizon latent feedback under a fixed mixed W/A memory budget, so this is an application-grade method rather than a claim of a new generic causal estimator.
BLOCK 3 — DESIGN RATIONALE
I kept one anchor and deferred the cross-backbone transfer gap and the standalone local-proxy audit because the former is a validation axis and the latter is a negative-control comparison that can run through the same anchor machinery; adding either as a separate leg would make the paper broader without making the method more complete.
I selected C00 from the anchor alternates because its fixed-model counterfactual measurement object directly matches candidate-order influence, while the heterogeneous companion turns that object into a runnable memory-constrained W/A allocation.
The method's central object is defined as a site-isolated estimand and the final full-model evaluation, not a claimed Shapley or globally additive optimum; this keeps the C00 compression step honest when joint quantization interactions violate additivity.
I rejected uniform local-reconstruction, generic activation-sensitivity, and module/horizon-only routes because they regress to the established n0-n2 PTQ lineage or repeat the n3 DINO-WM sweep; DINO-WM and LeFlow are full-text comparators, QuantWAMs is connector-verified but abstract-only and adjacent, and DreamerV3/TD-MPC2 remain explicitly inferred pilot anchors.
arxiv:2602.02110v1
It would have needed to hold candidate pools, planner inputs, receding-horizon conventions, and tie rules fixed while quantizing one site at a time, estimate that site's candidate-order discordance over action-conditioned rollout states, and solve the resulting profile as a memory-constrained mixed W/A assignment; the supplied method constructs PTQ sweeps and outcome analyses but not this decision-influence object or allocation step.

